# A call auction collects orders without matching them, then trades everything that crosses at a single price: the
# one that matches the most volume, then leaves the smallest surplus, then follows the side with the surplus.
> add ACME
added ACME
> auction begin
ACME call auction started: orders collect without matching
> buy 100 @ 100.30
ACME #1 BUY 100 @ 100.30 -> resting 100
> buy 50 @ 100.10
ACME #2 BUY 50 @ 100.10 -> resting 50
> sell 80 @ 99.90
ACME #3 SELL 80 @ 99.90 -> resting 80
> sell 60 @ 100.20
ACME #4 SELL 60 @ 100.20 -> resting 60

# The book may cross during the call. The indicative price shows what an uncross would do right now.
> book
ACME  AUCTION  last -  resting 4  indicative 100.20 x 100
              BID                       ASK
     100.30 x 100     (1)       99.90 x 80      (1)
     100.10 x 50      (1)      100.20 x 60      (1)
> auction show
ACME would uncross at 100.20: 100 matched, buy surplus 0, sell surplus 40

# Market orders take part with the highest priority.
> buy 40 market
ACME #5 BUY 40 @ market -> queued for the auction
> auction show
ACME would uncross at 100.20: 140 matched, buy surplus 0, sell surplus 0
> auction uncross
ACME uncrossed at 100.20: 140 matched
  trade #1  40 @ 100.20  buy #5  sell #3  (auction)
  trade #2  40 @ 100.20  buy #1  sell #3  (auction)
  trade #3  60 @ 100.20  buy #1  sell #4  (auction)
> book
ACME  CONTINUOUS  last 100.20  resting 1
              BID                       ASK
     100.10 x 50      (1)
> trades
  trade #3  60 @ 100.20  buy #1  sell #4  (auction)
  trade #2  40 @ 100.20  buy #1  sell #3  (auction)
  trade #1  40 @ 100.20  buy #5  sell #3  (auction)
